The core is healthy now. End-to-end data-plane tests from the edge succeeded: pc0 (20.1.1.10) to pc12 (120.1.1.10) shows 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT both directions. Control-plane across the central routers is stable: OSPF adjacencies on the “middle” devices r3, r4, r5, and r6 are all Full on their inter-core links (e.g., r3 neighbors over eth1/eth2/eth3/eth0; r4 over eth0/eth1/eth2; r5 over eth0/eth1/eth2; r6 over eth0/eth1/eth2). Their OSPF routing tables are fully populated with expected inter-area routes, and next-hops are consistent across the core. Interfaces on r3 are up with correct addressing, and no dead or stuck OSPF states were observed.

Mechanism: If a core router were down or a core link failed, we would see OSPF neighbors in Init/Down or LSA ageouts, missing routes, and end-to-end loss. Instead, adjacencies are Full and routes are present, so traffic forwards normally.

Localization: No single device or interface in the r3–r4–r5–r6 core shows faults. Edge domains (r1/r2 side and r8/r12 side) also have stable neighbors and routes.

Root cause: The overnight alert appears to have been a transient flap or false positive; there is no current core-router failure.

Corrective fix: No action required. Clear the alert. If this was intermittent, enable short-term OSPF event logging and interface error counters on r3–r6 to catch any brief flaps, and review monitoring thresholds to avoid spurious alarms. === END ===